# The repository stores text as LF, and every checkout gets LF back.
#
# Without this, git has no opinion and both machines impose their own: a
# Windows checkout writes CRLF, a Linux VM reaching the same tree over a mount
# writes LF, and the result is dozens of files reported as wholly rewritten in
# every session.  That is not cosmetic -- it means no commit can use a
# directory pathspec, because `git add shared/` sweeps in files whose only
# change is invisible.  The predecessor learned this the expensive way and
# settled it in a dedicated commit; here it is settled before the first file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text.  Git's own detection usually gets these right, but "usually" on a
# 15 KB golden screenshot means a corrupted image and a render check that
# fails for a reason nobody will guess.
*.png binary
*.uf2 binary
*.bin binary
